# Assets compiled into the binary with //go:embed must have the same bytes
# regardless of the platform the binary was built on.
#
# Without this, a checkout on Windows (where git's core.autocrlf defaults to
# true) rewrites these to CRLF, and the embed takes them as they are on disk —
# so a Windows-built release ships CRLF where a Linux-built one ships LF. For a
# Dockerfile that is merely inconsistent; for the standalone scripts it is not,
# since a Python .pth file or a shebang script with carriage returns fails inside
# the Linux container it is written into.
#
# Found by the first run of the sub-module tests on Windows: a scaffold test
# compared an embedded Dockerfile against a literal and got "\r\n" where the
# source file has "\n".
*.tmpl                          text eol=lf
airflow/include/**              text eol=lf
pkg/checks/**.py                text eol=lf
pkg/airflowrt/include/**        text eol=lf
pkg/airflowrt/standalone_scripts/** text eol=lf
pkg/localrt/internal/localdocker/*.tmpl text eol=lf
pkg/proxy/**.tmpl               text eol=lf
# The 1.x flag inventory is embedded and read line by line; with CRLF every
# line ended in "\r" and the CLI could not read it on a Windows build.
cmd/v1_flags.tsv                text eol=lf

# Shell scripts are executed as-is by CI and by the git hooks; CRLF makes them
# fail with an unreadable "bad interpreter" error.
*.sh                            text eol=lf

# Golden fixtures are compared byte-for-byte against generated output, and the
# generator emits LF. Left to git's platform default they arrive with CRLF on a
# Windows checkout and every comparison fails on line endings rather than on
# content — which is what the first Windows run of these tests actually reported.
**/testdata/**                  text eol=lf

# Read verbatim into requests that tests compare against LF literals (the
# git-sync ssh key, for one): the same CRLF problem as the goldens above.
cmd/apc/testfiles/**            text eol=lf
